{"title":"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A mathematical organization is producing a set of commemorative license plates. Each plate contains a sequence of five characters chosen from the four letters in AIME and the four digits in 2007. No character may appear in a sequence more times than it appears among the four letters in AIME or the four digits in 2007. A set of plates in which each possible sequence appears exactly once contains N license plates. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/3/9c34b06249aac069179cec1997539a6c74799bba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{N}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are 7 different characters that can be picked, with 0 being the only number that can be repeated twice.\n</p>\n<ul><li>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> appears 0 or 1 times amongst the sequence, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/6012868b59d84dec0de833209e2aba139d791714.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!} = 2520$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"123\" height=\"42\" /> sequences possible.</li>\n<li>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> appears twice in the sequence, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31cde4daadf72ce3272cd6b4993ad5bd9f5453d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${5\\choose2} = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"79\" height=\"53\" /> places to place the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/6/7e6e9383c620ba695aa589f65b030e057a38244a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6!}{(6-3)!} = 120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"114\" height=\"42\" /> ways to place the remaining three characters. In total, that gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/0192d475b8f7c326c6f1c4cfdd3c4cb5cccd6149.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 \\cdot 120 = 1200$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"118\" height=\"13\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70ee24580ebf9b161db80bd41891315a8efbfc22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N = 2520 + 1200 = 3720$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"195\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/7/2f7aeeca7efaece4e9abb3c2565bfe4e0af5c8f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{N}{10} = \\boxed{372}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"85\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Note: We could also count cases by how many 0's there are, like No 0's, 1 0's, and 2 0's. We get the same answer.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Another approach is to first falsely assume that the zeros are distinguishable and then correct the over-counting.\n</p><p>We can name the zeros <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/5/855ebca91d0e3094d092d1c6772026f4dbfc32e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/7/077d96d2320cd4b0bf938ab8beff654720d68d29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" />. The first character has 8 choices - <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/c/b0ca34339e2f49da734b762f45f70ba68cbd8eb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,I,M,E,2,0_1,0_2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"154\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. The second character has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choices, the third has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choices, the fourth has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choices, and the fifth has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choices. Multiplying them all together, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/6/0364e377c4049395947a741c334cc59ab36d1954.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6,720$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"17\" /> arrangements where the zeros are distinguishable.\n</p><p>However, some cases with at most 2 zeros are over-counted. We can tackle this by going case by case for the number of zeros. When there are no zeros there will be no arrangements over-counted. \n</p><p>When there is exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> zero, all the cases are over-counted. This is because there will be another case with the exact same characters but the zero will be switched with the other zero. The number of ways to place the zero is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> zeros to choose from, and the remaining spots have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/b/1fbbc2025ab3bcf59d39f155a206eaa27b5bf5a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"78\" height=\"14\" /> ways to place the non-zero characters. Thus, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/a/46af2450b73cfdf152b28e7bcf94baaf322dd036.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 = 3,600$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"191\" height=\"17\" /> arrangements where there is exactly one zero. However, we need to remove half of these or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/7/cd75c57566e60d168bead8e9cfe6ff95000ddbf1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,800$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"17\" /> from the total yielding in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/1/8017970307c02c6adf2eaec8d33b4ccf166ce382.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4,920$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"17\" /> arrangements left.\n</p><p>Cases with two zeros would also have to be corrected as the zeros are interchangeable and you can switch the ordering of them. The number of ways to place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/5/855ebca91d0e3094d092d1c6772026f4dbfc32e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the number of ways to place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/7/077d96d2320cd4b0bf938ab8beff654720d68d29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the remaining places have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/b/98b73f9ec4f260e2f7775454d920f2b551272fc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> ways to place the remaining characters. In total, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/a/d4a870774fe001e05ad240cf5b059af1b765cca6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 = 2,400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"168\" height=\"17\" /> arrangements with exactly two zeros. However, we need to remove half of these or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/6/6964422f751ba8835e9103e3574d4ec9d1025400.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> from the total yielding in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/1/ff150e4090aa2724340a20b6df84098a4144ae07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3,720$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"17\" /> arrangements left.\n</p><p>The max number of zeros is two so we have gone through all the cases and removed the over-counted arrangements thus giving, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f135c12424dd7f70bbb6dbcef4a3bdf384f149f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N = 3,720$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/7/2f7aeeca7efaece4e9abb3c2565bfe4e0af5c8f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{N}{10} = \\boxed{372}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"85\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>~ Logicalrhino65\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Another way to solve this is through brute force casework.\n</p><p>We can have 1, 2, 3, or 4 letters in the sequence, each from AIME. \n</p><p><b>Case 1: 4 letters</b>\n</p><p>Here, we have a string in the base form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/d/0ed0c51b8eeaf52e4f1e6366fd60a9f64c066702.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AIMEx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"69\" height=\"14\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/5/775f1343b4b5ede2dfb0594717b08846c5bd150e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\{0,2,7 \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"96\" height=\"20\" />. That is three ways times <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/2933f407c21ceb3f3ec8370f43cd5b939250ffca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> permutations, being 360 cases.\n</p><p><b>Case 2: 3 letters</b>\n</p><p>Assume the string has base form with no zeroes. Then, it takes the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/e/abebb7b442bc6b355c76508d97f1a7181164154e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AIM27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" />, which has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/2933f407c21ceb3f3ec8370f43cd5b939250ffca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> permutations, times the 4 ways to choose the three letters, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/5/b95e6c4db444ab5d1bd6e9756b1c636d04de2857.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5! \\cdot 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />. Say it has one zero. Then, it takes the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/4/9d49ba6fcd18c08aa5a1003e82012e949ad94e01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AIM0x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/1/fc1a7a7d49725f12df100473e2f95534fe8f3658.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\{2,7 \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/b/7bb66036ad70063be6b9e3749b52428e9bf68dc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot  5!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />. Otherwise, the string has two zeroes, and that is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/5/c456cf67a60b347a3f47ab94eeb6183b18bd7fd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AIM00$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/b/e5b530299a9efd26d112461d51d35d7a8531e782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\cdot \\frac{5!}{2!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"42\" height=\"39\" />. Summing, we have 1680 cases.\n</p><p><b>Case 3: 2 letters</b>\n</p><p>No base form with no zeroes exists. Therefore, the base form has one zero. Then it takes the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/c/4bca9d3cd52c740fa9dc88fd1ed7abc455303645.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AI207$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/4/4e4ce90157416b5aae29e67adfa422abb100f1d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\cdot 5!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> ways. Otherwise, it has two zeroes, and the base form is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/7/04782336c7ced312e23e17178c734d9bff776433.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AI00x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/1/fc1a7a7d49725f12df100473e2f95534fe8f3658.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\{2,7 \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" />. Then, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/8/b787b64636b2c868a3008863a5faf5995d9610cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5!}{2!} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"64\" height=\"39\" /> cases here. Summing, we have 1440 cases.\n</p><p><b>Case 4: 1 letter</b>\n</p><p>The number 2007 must be in the string. Otherwise, we have 4 choices for the letter, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/d/a8d954d8b313ad69b9f15f10e411c67501183645.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5!}{2!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"39\" /> total arrangements. Then, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/5/eb537d7a389bf5907cf9006e04b2d2b403c9d50e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5!}{2!} \\cdot 4 = 240$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"39\" /> cases.\n</p><p>Every subcase here ends in a 0, so when the total count <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is divided by 10, we just remove the 0's in the subcases. The requested <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/5/92591b67124ed0c6114c011efb8a97e262d75cad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24 + 144 + 168+36 = \\boxed{372}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"222\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II\" title=\"2007 AIME II\">2007 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2007 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2007\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First Question</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2007 AIME II Problems/Problem 9\"